From 75b5df2d211d5b85564ea33a44ad27b9af2fe8e0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 7 Aug 2026 06:50:19 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] Add an email signup to the More screen, feeding
 EmailOctopus
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The app was the one surface with no way to join the list. It now has one, on
More, posting to a new subscribe function that adds the address to the same
single EmailOctopus list the websites feed.

It does not navigate. The three websites use plain Netlify forms that post and
land on a thanks page; this is an installed app, and bouncing someone out to a
confirmation screen is how an app stops feeling like one. The form is real, so
Return on the phone keyboard works, but the submit is intercepted and the card
repaints in place. Offline is checked before the fetch rather than after it
fails, because out here offline is the normal case and "you are offline" is
worth saying where "that did not work" just looks broken.

Signups are tagged `app` and `source-app-more`, with no brand tag. The app is
all three brands at once and picking one would be a guess.

Unlike the websites' equivalent — which Netlify calls itself and nothing
outside can reach — this endpoint is public, so it carries a honeypot, checks
the address itself rather than trusting type=email, and refuses anything but
POST. No rate limit: the upsert is idempotent, so flooding it produces one
contact and some log noise rather than a mess or a bill.

_lib/emailoctopus.mjs is a copy of the farmhousegetaways file. Two separate
Netlify sites, and neither repo wants npm just to share one module, so the
copies are kept in step by hand and `npm test` guards the part that would fail
silently: tags go to the API as an object map, and an array is accepted,
ignored, and leaves every contact untagged.

The other HTML files change only because the CSS hash moved.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
